900: exactly in \path{code/part3_fourfold/direct_section.py} and its recorded
901: outputs.
902: Proper cohomology and base change give the vanishing below.
903: More precisely, Stacks Project Lemma 30.22.1, tag 07VK,
904: applies to this proper flat projective elliptic fibration and
905: its line bundle: on each Noetherian affine base open it provides
906: a perfect complex computing the direct image and commuting with
907: arbitrary derived base change.  Every fibre complex is zero by
908: the preceding calculation.  A bounded complex of finite projective
909: modules with this property is zero: at any local ring, cancel
910: every differential entry that is a unit; the remaining minimal
911: complex has zero differentials after reduction to the residue
912: field, so its zero fibre cohomology forces every remaining rank
913: to be zero.  This proves
914: 
915: \[
916:  R f_*(M+nF)=0.
917: \]
918: 
919: Consequently the Dolbeault index is zero.  Combining the actual
920: symbol factorization and cap comparison gives
921: 
922: \[
923:  A=\operatorname{ind}(D_{\bar X,b})
924:   =\operatorname{ind}\bar\partial_{S,M+nF}=0.
925: \]
926: 
927: The computation uses fibrewise acyclicity and the explicit cap
928: switch; it does not reuse the numerical page characteristic
929: calculation in P3K.7.  The integral cover argument P3F.11 again
930: gives zero for the original parameter return.
931: \end{proof}
932: 
933: \begin{statement}{P3K.9}{Proved}
934: For the rotating framing specified below, the one-gamma-turn
935: return has Jin characteristic numbers \((k,\ell)=(-6,-24)\).
936: After removing an extending constant-rotation loop, this framing
937: descends through the sixfold parameter cover.  With the descended
938: framing the original positive return has
939: 
940: \[
941:  k=-1,\qquad \ell=-4.
942: \]
943: 
944: Every other tubular framing differs by the explicitly described
945: changes \((k,\ell)\mapsto(k+n,\ell+4n)\) of P3F.11.  The asserted
946: individual values include the framing; their index is independent
947: of that choice.
948: \end{statement}
949: 
950: \begin{proof}
951: \par\medskip\noindent\textbf{Frames on the two pieces.}\enspace Give the graph copy of the page
952: its normal frame consisting of its oriented normal in \(Y\),
953: followed by the two Cartesian parameter directions.  The graph
954: derivative changes this frame only by a triangular shear, whose
955: off-diagonal block contracts linearly to zero.  On the compression
956: use a normal frame of \(W\subset Y\times I\), then transport it
957: by the actual gamma rotation \(R_\theta\).  This normal rank-three
958: bundle is trivial.  Indeed \(W\) is a solid torus minus a ball and
959: has trivial tangent bundle, while \(TY\oplus\mathbf R\) is trivial;
960: the Whitney formula gives \(w_2(\nu W)=0\).  Over a three-complex,
961: an oriented rank-three bundle is classified by \(w_2\), since
962: \(\pi_1(SO(3))=\mathbf Z/2\) and \(\pi_2(SO(3))=0\).
963: At the sphere end, its comparison with the fixed positive-Hopf
964: frame is a map \(S^2\to SO(3)\).  This extends over \(D^3\) because
965: \(\pi_2(SO(3))=0\).  Make this extension at parameter zero and
966: transport it by \(R_\theta\).  The resulting frame extends over
967: the whole marked compression cap \(H_a\).
968: 
969: We compute the transition between these two actual frames.  Use
970: radian angular coordinates in this paragraph.  Let \(\phi\) be
971: the fixed outward-collar phase, namely the Milnor-page phase plus
972: \(\pi\), and let \(d=12\) be its degree along the gamma circle.
973: At the binding its outward page ray has angle
974: \(\alpha=\phi-d\gamma\) in the complex \(q-\epsilon\) plane.
975: The exact graph boundary from P3K.6 is
976: \(\gamma=\theta+g_\epsilon(p)\), where
977: \(p\in T^2\) is the source point and \(g_\epsilon\) is now the
978: radian-valued lift of that graph.  No source-dependent change of
979: the binding phase is made.
980: 
981: After quotienting by the source tangent directions and the tangent
982: to this boundary graph, the four remaining coordinates are the
983: real and imaginary \(q-\epsilon\) directions, the parameter radial
984: direction, and the relative angular direction.  The chosen page
985: collar has positive parameter radial derivative, pointing from the
986: page towards the compression.  We use the outward page collar and
987: the inward compression tangent with this same direction.  In the
988: four indicated coordinates its tangent is
989: \(u=(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha,\rho,0)\), with \(\rho>0\).
990: The explicit quotient morphism, from this four-space modulo
991: \(\mathbf Ru\) to the reference three-space, is
992: 
993: \[
994:  (v_q,v_r,v_\eta)\longmapsto
995:  \left(v_q-\frac{v_r}{\rho}(\cos\alpha,\sin\alpha),v_\eta\right).
996: \]
997: 
998: The Cartesian parameter axes have radial-relative coordinates
999: \((\cos\theta,-\sin\theta)\) and
1000: \((\sin\theta,\cos\theta)\), after a positive scaling of the
1001: relative angular coordinate.  Project them by this formula,
1002: reverse both parameter axes, and apply the positive reference
1003: rescaling \(\operatorname{diag}(\rho,\rho,1)\); normalize the
1004: page-normal column by the positive factor \(\rho^{-1}\).
1005: The graph normal frame in
1006: this reference space, after constant oriented changes of frame and
1007: positive rescalings, is
1008: 
1009: \[
1010:  G(\alpha,\theta)=
1011:  \begin{pmatrix}
1012:  -\sin\alpha&\cos\theta\cos\alpha&\sin\theta\cos\alpha\\
1013:  \cos\alpha&\cos\theta\sin\alpha&\sin\theta\sin\alpha\\
1014:  0&\sin\theta&-\cos\theta
1015:  \end{pmatrix}.
1016: \]
1017: 
1018: Here the first column is the oriented page normal, and the other
1019: two columns are the Cartesian parameter directions projected along
1020: the collar tangent.  The coefficients of the source derivatives
1021: of \(g_\epsilon\) enter only the source tangent directions already
1022: quotiented out; they give triangular shears before this quotient.
1023: The positive radial collar coefficient can be rescaled to one.
1024: During collar rounding the transverse vector remains in the same
1025: open radial half-space.  This space is contractible, so this
1026: rounding contributes no additional three-dimensional clutching
1027: class.  These operations specify a homotopy of the actual normal
1028: transition, not merely an equality of its characteristic numbers.
1029: 
1030: Direct multiplication gives \(G\in SO(3)\) and
1031: \(G(\alpha,\theta)=R_z(\alpha)G(0,\theta)\), where \(R_z\)
1032: rotates the first two reference axes.  The compression-cap frame
1033: in these same coordinates has the form \(h(p)\), independent of
1034: \(\theta\): both the ambient product coordinates and this frame
1035: are transported by \(R_\theta\).  Consequently the full transition
1036: on the marked \(T^3\) is homotopic to
1037: 
1038: \[
1039: \begin{aligned}
1040:  h(p)^{-1}G(\phi-d(\theta+g_\epsilon(p)),\theta)
1041:  &=\bigl[h(p)^{-1}R_z(-d g_\epsilon(p))\bigr]\\
1042:  &\quad\cdot\bigl[R_z(\phi-d\theta)G(0,\theta)\bigr].
1043: \end{aligned}
1044: \]
1045: 
1046: The first factor is pulled back from the source \(T^2\), and the
1047: second from the parameter circle.  The cubic Maurer--Cartan form
1048: of a product of two maps is the sum of their cubic forms plus an
1049: exact form; this identity follows by expanding
1050: \((ab)^{-1}d(ab)=b^{-1}(a^{-1}da)b+b^{-1}db\) and using the
1051: Maurer--Cartan equations.  Each of the two cubic forms here is
1052: zero by dimension.  Its integral over \(T^3\) is therefore zero.
1053: The Pontryagin clutching formula can be obtained directly by using
1054: the interpolating connection \(t g^{-1}dg\) on the collar between
1055: two framed pieces: its Chern--Weil integral is a fixed multiple
1056: of that cubic integral.  We conclude
1057: 
1058: \[
1059:  \langle p_1(\bar\nu),[\bar X]\rangle=0.
1060: \]
1061: 
1062: This calculation allows nontrivial one-dimensional and
1063: two-dimensional frame data.  It proves the required vanishing
1064: without replacing the actual normal bundle by a trivial bundle.
1065: The matrix identities and ensuing exact arithmetic are exported
1066: in \path{code/part3_fourfold/normal_clutch.py} and its JSON and text outputs.
1067: 
1068: \par\medskip\noindent\textbf{The integers and their descent.}\enspace P3K.7 now gives, with
1069: its full orientation comparison,
1070: 
1071: \[
1072:  k_{\gamma}=\frac{0+3(-8)}4=-6,
1073:  \qquad \ell_{\gamma}=-24+0=-24.
1074: \]
1075: 
1076: We must compare the rotating framing to framings pulled back from
1077: one original parameter circuit.  The fundamental group of the
1078: normal-frame gauge space is
1079: 
1080: \[
1081:  \pi_1\operatorname{Map}(S^2,SO(3))
1082:  =\pi_1(SO(3))\oplus\pi_3(SO(3))
1083:  =\mathbf Z/2\oplus\mathbf Z.
1084: \]
1085: 
1086: Evaluation at one point, with its constant-map section, gives the
1087: displayed splitting.  Pullback through the positive sixfold circle
1088: cover sends \((\varepsilon,n)\) to \((0,6n)\).  Choose any
1089: framing over the original parameter circle and write \(k_0\) for
1090: its Jin integer.  Its sixfold pullback has integer \(6k_0\).
